// fixture-226: S4a — S3 seed plus cross-pkg enable convergence (R3-C regression)
//
// Tests that the S4a fixpoint converges correctly when a dep (lib-target) has
// flags seeded via BOTH:
//   - S3 consumer-side flag request: lib-consumer depends on lib-target with
//     { flag "feat-x" }, which seeds dep_active_flags[lib-target]={feat-x}
//     during the initial BFS fetch phase.
//   - S4a cross-pkg enable: lib-enabler has enabler-flag (default=true) that
//     fires enables { lib-target { flag "feat-y" } }.
//
// The bug (R3-C): the S4a convergence check compared new_active.keys() (only
// the current iteration's flags, e.g. {feat-y}) against old_flag_names (the
// full union from the store, e.g. {feat-x, feat-y}).  Since {feat-y} != {feat-x,
// feat-y} every iteration, the fixpoint never converged → MILPA-INTERNAL crash.
//
// Python was correct: it computed merged = old ∪ new_active FIRST, then compared
// frozenset(merged.keys()) == old_flag_names (the POST-UNION set both sides).
//
// After fix: Rust mirrors Python — compute the union locally (old_flag_names ∪
// new_active.keys()), use the union for convergence check AND edge admission
// (removing the R2 read-back-from-store smell, Finding-5).
//
// lib-target admits lib-end only when BOTH feat-x AND feat-y are active.
// Expected resolved graph: lib-consumer + lib-enabler + lib-target + lib-end.
// lib-target.active_flags must include both "feat-x" and "feat-y".
name "myapp"
kind "application"
deps {
    lib-consumer git=(url)"https://example.com/lib-consumer.git" ref="main"
    lib-enabler git=(url)"https://example.com/lib-enabler.git" ref="main"
}
